WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.988 --> 00:00:07.280
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:11.520
Today we'll talk about
what's new with BigQuery ML,

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.930
and we also have
Demandbase with us

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:18.390
to talk about their
usage of BigQuery ML.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:22.650
I'm Abhishek Kashyap, the
Product Manager for BigQuery ML

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.250
and Data QnA.

00:00:25.868 --> 00:00:26.910
ERIC WHITE: And I'm Eric.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.320
I'm a data scientist,
and I'll be

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.950
discussing two ways
that Demandbase

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.723
leverages BigQuery ML.

00:00:31.723 --> 00:00:33.390
My colleague, Leo
Benkel, was originally

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.800
slated to present with me.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.487
He did a lot of the
work behind this talk,

00:00:37.487 --> 00:00:39.570
so I'd just like to note
that I will be presenting

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:40.907
his slides on his behalf.

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:42.990
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Thank
you, Eric, for being here.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.110
We'll start with talking
about what's new,

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:52.740
and then Eric will talk
about Demandbase use cases

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.590
with BigQuery ML.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:02.070
To summarize, we have added new
types of models to BigQuery ML

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:08.370
to help you create many
more machine learning models

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:13.200
to address most of the use cases
that you would have for machine

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.790
learning in BigQuery.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:19.140
Specifically, for classification
and regression models,

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.480
we have added the support
for creation of boosted trees

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.260
using XGBoost, deep
neural networks using

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.650
TensorFlow, and AutoML tables.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.590
In terms of other
use cases, we have

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.530
added support for creation
of hundreds of thousands

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:41.820
of time series models
with a single SQL command,

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.440
and you can also now create
product recommendation systems

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.270
using matrix factorization.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.760
For use cases that
require online prediction,

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.810
you can now export any
model created in BigQuery ML

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:00.740
and use it for online prediction
in Google Cloud AI platform,

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.530
or your own serving stack.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.009
To set the stage, I'll spend a
minute talking about BigQuery.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:13.460
BigQuery is Google Cloud's
enterprise data warehouse.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.280
We have exabytes of
data stored in BigQuery,

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.820
and customers are able to
run SQL queries on petabytes

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.050
of data.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:25.130
All of this is enabled by
the serverless architecture

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:28.550
of BigQuery, and
underlying that is

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.420
the separation of storage
and compute, which

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.590
has allowed our largest
customers to store

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.820
more than 250 petabytes
of data in BigQuery,

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.910
and the largest
queries being run

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:46.060
are around five
petabytes of data.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.920
Now, this is awesome
for data analytics.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.050
However, when you want to do
machine learning on this data,

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.380
we see a lot of tasks
which are not really

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.420
machine learning-specific.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:03.710
Let's say you create a model in
a notebook on a sample of data.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:07.730
That works well, but to
create a production model

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:12.230
we see the need to write ETL
pipelines for model creation,

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:16.730
and then you also have to do
ETL pipelines for predictions,

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:17.690
as well.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.630
Now, bringing data
to machine learning

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:23.390
carries that list of tasks.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:27.650
The bigger headache, however,
is that all of the governance

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.560
policies that have been
defined on the data just

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.040
go out of the window
the second you export it

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.540
between a model on a VM cluster
outside the query, which

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:42.650
I'm sure no enterprise likes.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.020
We are solving that problem
through a SQL interface

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.590
to machine learning
called BigQuery ML.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:52.200
With BigQuery ML,
we are bringing

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.170
machine learning
to the data itself

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.050
through two simple SQL commands.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:02.060
One, you can say
"create model," and that

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:06.380
would build a model for
you within BigQuery.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:11.970
And second, you can just say
ML.predict to use that model

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.930
and get predictions in
BigQuery All your features go

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.790
into the Select Statement,
so you can very, very fast

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:22.320
create models by
changing your features

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.800
and the model type
within that SQL.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.920
We do automate a lot
of standard ML tasks,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:32.070
like split-off training
and test data sets,

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.350
standardization of
numeric features

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.640
and coding of
categorical features.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.850
So with a minimal
number of lines of code,

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.730
you can get a pretty good model.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:45.540
And finally, since this
happens in BigQuery,

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.610
there is no
infrastructure management,

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:51.165
and all the governance and
access policies you defined

00:04:51.165 --> 00:04:54.300
are automatically applied
to these machine learning

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:57.700
workloads as well.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:01.420
To recap, we are
extending BigQuery ML

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:05.230
by support of all these
new model types, which

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.420
should enable you
to address most

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.550
of the machine
learning use cases you

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:11.910
have on BigQuery data.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:16.870
To give you an example
with a time series model,

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:20.050
one of our developer
advocates, Felipe Hoffa,

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:26.380
wanted to predict how many Stack
Overflow tags would be there

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:28.120
for each of those.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:32.910
And while doing that, Felipe
created 15,000 different times

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:36.490
series models in 20 minutes.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:39.670
Each of those is hypertuned,
so we actually ended up

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:43.690
creating 600,000 models
within 20 minutes,

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:45.730
without any
infrastructure management,

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:49.260
without any data ETL.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.550
Another example of a
capability we have brought on

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:55.280
is the addition
of AutoML tables.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:00.140
AutoML tables was created to
build a best-in-class machine

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:02.660
learning model on
structured data,

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:05.690
without any feature
engineering, and without having

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:09.280
to think about which
network architecture to use.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.610
AutoML tables searches
through a set of architectures

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:16.370
and builds the best
possible model for you.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:21.480
AutoML tables has been
available to its own UI,

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:26.570
and now we have added it as
a model type to BigQuery ML.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:30.080
So just like you can
create an [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.630
model or a logistic
regression model,

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:36.110
you can now create an
AutoML table classifier

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:39.830
through the same SQL
interface, and very easily

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.760
build it into your data and
machine learning pipelines.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:51.870
Now, all these are being
used for a set of use cases

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:54.330
by many of our customers.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:58.960
To give you a few examples of
the most popular use cases,

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:01.810
let's start with
the retail industry.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:06.400
The use cases we are seeing are
creating product recommendation

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:10.990
systems to send products
through emails, which

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:14.050
are very personalized
for the end users,

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:17.050
calculating the propensity
to purchase or propensity

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:21.080
to click on an asset for
each customer, and again,

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.710
using it in marketing campaigns.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:27.400
If you want to forecast
demand of a particular product

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.670
or category by store or by
region, with time series,

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:33.100
you can now do that in
a single SQL command

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.370
and use it for better
merchandising and supply chain

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:38.340
optimization.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:42.810
And the fourth one is
doing customer analytics

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:46.360
by easily creating
customer segments, a.k.a.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.450
Personas.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.840
When we look at
logistics and CPG,

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:55.530
the use cases we are seeing
are estimation of the time

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.220
a package will be
delivered, or when

00:07:57.220 --> 00:07:59.400
a vehicle will get somewhere--

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.360
predictive
maintenance, prediction

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:04.280
of how much inventory
of a product

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:07.310
would be needed at
a certain warehouse.

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:12.710
For financial services and
insurance, on the retail side,

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:15.160
we are seeing usage
in calculation

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:19.210
of customer LTV, which can
then underlie all business

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:21.610
decisions for each customer.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:24.310
We can improve the
underwriting process

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:28.570
by easily predicting loan
default probability at the time

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:31.210
of receiving an application.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:35.380
You can use BigQuery ML for
the batch credit card fraud

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:39.200
with fraud detection
pipelines that run overnight.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.659
And finally, there are use
cases in anti-money laundering,

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:47.215
as well, where you can
forecast how many transactions

00:08:47.215 --> 00:08:52.350
may be stopped for AML checks.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:56.380
The fourth vertical, which
is media, gaming, high tech,

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:59.310
has use cases similar
to retail, where

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:03.810
you want to recommend content
to an app on the website.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:07.000
Or you want to predict
player activity,

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.400
you want to win a
game difficulty.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:12.070
Similarly, you may
want to predict

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:13.870
if someone is
going to subscribe,

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:16.330
or if someone is going
to churn, and take

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:18.320
a marketing action accordingly.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:23.800
Now, all these use cases can
be solved through BigQuery ML

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.440
with the new features
we have added.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:31.370
To walk you through an
example, a lot of our customers

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:35.420
join data from multiple
online and offline sources,

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:39.620
and create a Customer
360 profile in BigQuery.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:42.990
Now, this Customer
360 profile primarily

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:46.310
contains behavior
data, transaction data,

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.590
and demographic data.

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:51.605
You can now use all
of these together

00:09:51.605 --> 00:09:56.240
to create a set of customer
segments right within BigQuery

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.370
by using kmeans
clustering, and we

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:00.440
have customers who are
doing it on hundreds

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:03.310
of millions of their customers.

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:07.010
And once you have those, you
can build recommendation systems

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:10.340
in place and run
personalized campaigns

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:14.630
to each of these customers,
again, staying within BigQuery

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:20.680
and using BigQuery ML for
these machine learning tasks.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:24.910
In terms of pricing, if you
are a flat rate customer

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:30.310
or you use flex slots, you
can use your slots as-is.

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:31.870
There is no additional charge.

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:34.420
Each time you build
a model, we just

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:37.000
use the slots you have
already purchased.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:40.510
If you are using auto limited
tables or a model type which

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:43.440
uses cloud AI platform
in the back end,

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:48.670
we convert those resources
into slots at price parity.

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:53.020
If you use BigQuery On Demand,
the cloud AI platform cost

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:56.410
is built as a
pass-through to BigQuery,

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:59.730
so there is price
parity there, as well.

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:01.630
For models which are
trained natively,

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:05.620
like time series, matrix
factorization, and regression,

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:09.650
the pricing is according to
the amount of training data

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:12.950
that we use.

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:15.747
With that, I'll
pass it on to Eric.

00:11:15.747 --> 00:11:16.955
ERIC WHITE: Thanks, Abhishek.

00:11:16.955 --> 00:11:19.900
And thanks to Google for
giving us the chance to speak.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:23.590
Again, I'm an Eric, and I'm a
data scientist at Demandbase.

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:27.460
I'll be discussing two use cases
where we leverage BigQuery ML.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:29.722
The first involves our
digital advertising products,

00:11:29.722 --> 00:11:31.930
which consists of training
a model on massive amounts

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:33.760
of data at scale.

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:35.440
The second use case
has to do with how

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.160
we took a fairly complicated
Spark model that runs on demand

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:41.860
and converted it to
run in BigQuery ML,

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:45.630
and ended up with quite a bit of
savings, both in time and cost.

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.970
So I'll provide a little more
context for each use case

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.091
before diving in.

00:11:50.091 --> 00:11:52.960
But first, a little
bit about Demandbase.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:54.320
We're a late stage startup.

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:57.140
We have offices in San
Francisco, Seattle, New York,

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:59.720
and London, and we have
raised over $100 million

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:01.280
in venture capital.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:03.650
We consider ourselves
an AI-first company.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:05.150
We process the
world's business data

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:07.550
to help marketing and
sales organizations,

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:11.150
and our customers include over
100 Fortune 500 companies.

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:13.100
We offer a SaaS platform
for orchestrating

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:15.170
account-based marketing
strategies, which

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:17.570
includes a targeting
solution for advertising,

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:21.110
an engagement solution for site
optimization and customization,

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:23.750
and a conversion tool to align
ABM initiatives with sales

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:24.665
teams.

00:12:24.665 --> 00:12:26.540
We have a strong data
science and engineering

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:31.572
team, all of whom are quite
active in the GCP ecosystem.

00:12:31.572 --> 00:12:33.280
So the first use case
I'd like to discuss

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:35.140
involves our targeting
solution, which

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:38.540
is effectively a B2B digital
advertising platform.

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:40.820
We use AI to optimize
delivery of digital media

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:43.460
to all accounts in our
customers' ad campaigns.

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:44.870
We provide full
transparency when

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:47.250
it comes to advertising
metrics, such as media spend,

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:49.930
click-through rates, account
reach, et cetera, which

00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:51.680
means we're held
accountable to delivering

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:53.450
the highest-quality
inventory that

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:56.840
leads to clicks, engagement,
conversions, and so forth.

00:12:56.840 --> 00:13:00.130
We process roughly 430
billion options per month,

00:13:00.130 --> 00:13:02.450
and we've bid on those
auctions around the clock--

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:05.330
an average of 50,000 bids
per minute, which means we're

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:08.690
bidding on nearly a thousand
auctions every second.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:11.500
So just to give you a sense
of the size of this data,

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:14.100
again, we're bidding on nearly
a thousand requests per second,

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:17.720
and each bid request consists
of thousands of features.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:20.380
For example, this person here
visits her favorite news site

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:21.670
during the day.

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:23.268
We know the IP address
of the request.

00:13:23.268 --> 00:13:25.810
We know the company she works
for, right down to the location

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:27.208
of the office and geo.

00:13:27.208 --> 00:13:28.750
Whatever site she's
visiting, we know

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:30.340
the publisher, the
amount of inventory

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:33.207
on that page, the position
of the ad, et cetera.

00:13:33.207 --> 00:13:35.290
And finally, there's all
kinds of additional data,

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:37.570
such as the exchange,
the type of auction,

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:39.840
Deal ID, and so forth.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:42.570
On top of all that, we could
have dozens of our own customer

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:44.880
campaigns that qualify
to serve that single ad

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.160
during that visit, so
we also need to work out

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:48.398
which ones to serve.

00:13:48.398 --> 00:13:50.190
To do so, we use a
click-through rate model

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:52.140
that essentially takes
all these features

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:54.652
and predicts just how
valuable that option is,

00:13:54.652 --> 00:13:56.110
what the likelihood
is that it will

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:58.363
lead to a click, et cetera.

00:13:58.363 --> 00:14:00.030
And this scoring and
decision-making all

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:02.340
has to happen within several
milliseconds for each bid

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.985
request.

00:14:04.985 --> 00:14:06.360
So initially, we
did all our data

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:08.250
science in local
Jupyter notebooks,

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:09.880
meaning we'd export
the data out of

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:13.050
BigQuery in a Python notebook
for analysis, training,

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:15.930
validation, et cetera
in scikit-learn.

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:18.330
Besides being inconvenient,
limitations on the size

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:21.780
of the data, CPU, memory, meant
that we had to down-sample

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:22.410
the data--

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:24.600
not just the number of
rows, but even the number

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:26.700
of features that were
considered for the model.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:28.500
So it's no surprise that
with such a high cardinality

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.960
of features that this would
affect both the calibration

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:32.910
of the model and its accuracy.

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.220
Since our data already
resided in BigQuery,

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:38.310
we decided to use BigQuery
ML, namely because it solves

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:40.630
these issues and,
as I'll show next,

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:43.570
training a logistic regression
model is just trivially easy.

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:45.320
You just point your
model at your training

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:48.420
set, no matter how
many rows or features,

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.690
and BigQuery ML takes care
of [INAUDIBLE] coding,

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:54.210
k-fold cross-validation, and
everything else under the hood.

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:56.740
So it was much more
convenient, much faster,

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:59.785
and because we no longer had to
down-sample rows or features,

00:14:59.785 --> 00:15:01.410
we actually saw
significant improvement

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:04.820
in the calibration of the
model and its performance.

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:07.520
So just to show
you a real example,

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:09.840
this is how you train a
logistic regression model.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:12.590
It's really as simple is
writing a short SQL query.

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:15.367
You can see the table name
is bid_requests.training_set.

00:15:15.367 --> 00:15:16.950
You name the model
whatever you want--

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:19.160
in this case, model_name.

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:20.390
Specify the model type--

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:22.370
logistic regression,
in this case--

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:25.502
add a parameter for L1
regularization, and that's it.

00:15:25.502 --> 00:15:26.960
When it's done,
you save the output

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:28.800
just as you would any table.

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:30.710
And that table, which
is really a model,

00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:32.300
can then be queried
for validation,

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:34.313
or to make actual predictions.

00:15:34.313 --> 00:15:36.230
There are a variety of
things you can control.

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:38.290
If you want to do
multi-class classification,

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:40.520
or if you want to standardize
or scale your features,

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:41.752
it's all available.

00:15:41.752 --> 00:15:42.710
So this is what we did.

00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:44.620
This is how we moved
off of Scikit Learn

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:47.520
and onto BigQuery ML.

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:49.770
So what if you wanted to
try a different model,

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:52.920
let's say a deep neural
network, for example?

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.330
Almost nothing changes
except for the model type,

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:57.270
and in this case, how
many layers you want.

00:15:57.270 --> 00:16:00.510
Other than a physical limitation
of 512 megabytes, I believe,

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:03.002
you can have as many layers
and nodes as you wish.

00:16:03.002 --> 00:16:04.710
So in this case, I
specified three layers

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:09.140
with 256 nodes, and two more
layers with 128 nodes each,

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:09.930
and that's it.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:12.365
Just press Enter and wait
for the training to finish.

00:16:12.365 --> 00:16:13.740
So with deep neural
networks, you

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:16.237
have similar options
as logistic regression,

00:16:16.237 --> 00:16:18.570
but also ones specific to
neural networks, such as batch

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:22.090
size, dropout rate, et cetera.

00:16:22.090 --> 00:16:25.600
We also tried XGBoost, which is
sort of the hottest algorithm

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.550
around since random forest.

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:30.370
Again, you can see that very
little is changing here.

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:33.433
Same training data set,
just a different model type.

00:16:33.433 --> 00:16:35.350
In addition to parameters
specific to XGBoost,

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:37.060
such as the booster
type, it also

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:40.262
supports L1, L2 regularization,
and other options.

00:16:40.262 --> 00:16:41.845
There's even a boost
to tree regressor

00:16:41.845 --> 00:16:44.560
if you want to do regression
instead of classification.

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:46.150
Just change the model type.

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:48.680
So we've seen how to train
three different models.

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:53.710
How do we validate them
once they've been saved?

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:56.900
The answer is, once again,
as simple as a SQL query.

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:58.880
You've saved your
model with some name.

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:01.970
Just select the precision
recall F1 score from that model,

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:03.880
just as you would query a table.

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:06.200
This makes it very easy to
inspect validation metrics,

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:08.026
understand feature
importance and what

00:17:08.026 --> 00:17:09.859
was important to us was
being able to access

00:17:09.859 --> 00:17:11.460
the coefficients of our model.

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:13.960
What's really cool is that these
validation metrics can also

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:16.293
be viewed as interactive
dashboards that are built right

00:17:16.293 --> 00:17:17.440
into BigQuery UI.

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:19.035
So yeah, it's very cool stuff.

00:17:19.035 --> 00:17:20.950
And I should mention
that the XGBoost model

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:22.599
did have the best F1 score--

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:25.374
ever so slightly better than
logistic regression and DNN,

00:17:25.374 --> 00:17:28.569
but overall comparable F1
scores across all three models.

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:33.540
So the second use
case is for a solution

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:35.220
we call account selection.

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:36.750
Basically, a
customer provides us

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:39.120
with data, such as keywords,
describing their business,

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:41.950
buyer titles, or a list
of existing customers.

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:44.160
We use AI to identify
companies with strong buying

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:46.010
signals for that customer.

00:17:46.010 --> 00:17:48.710
Our account selection model
trains terabytes of B2B signals

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:50.460
and intent data, and
effectively generates

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:53.010
a ranked list of accounts
that your company should

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:54.318
target next.

00:17:54.318 --> 00:17:55.860
Because our customers
will prioritize

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:57.485
their entire marketing
and sales cycles

00:17:57.485 --> 00:17:59.970
with this list of companies,
it's incredibly important

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:01.198
that we get this right.

00:18:01.198 --> 00:18:03.240
Customers should be able
to update their profiles

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:05.550
and relaunch account selection,
which basically trains

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:07.592
a new model and generates
a new list of companies

00:18:07.592 --> 00:18:09.080
for that customer.

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:11.750
The way we initially saw this
was to build a model in Spark

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:15.140
and use on demand clusters,
which were not only costly,

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.150
but would cause issues
when the job failed,

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:18.530
because a new cluster
had to be provisioned

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:20.072
from the marketplace,
which increased

00:18:20.072 --> 00:18:23.400
the time and the
cost, et cetera.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:25.260
So a new model has to
be trained whenever

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:26.730
a user pushes the button.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:28.147
This means that
we have to support

00:18:28.147 --> 00:18:30.330
hundreds, if not thousands,
of jobs per week,

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:33.510
and the cost was something
like dozens of dollars per job.

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:36.150
In terms of size, the features
include intent keywords,

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:39.623
company firmographics, job
titles, and other B2B signals.

00:18:39.623 --> 00:18:41.040
Basically, thousands
of dimensions

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:44.240
that we reduced using techniques
like matrix factorization.

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:46.980
We previously stored our data
in all kinds of disparate data

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:50.280
sources, from Postgres to
static files in the Cloud.

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:51.900
In terms of time,
starting a cluster

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:53.520
and then training
the Spark model

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:56.443
typically took up to 40 minutes
for each job to complete.

00:18:56.443 --> 00:18:58.110
And when a job would
fail, a new cluster

00:18:58.110 --> 00:18:59.340
would have to be started.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:01.715
We would occasionally take
several hours for a single job

00:19:01.715 --> 00:19:04.220
to complete.

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:06.610
So along comes BigQuery ML.

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:09.460
We decided to replace
our Spark random forest

00:19:09.460 --> 00:19:12.940
model with a logistic
regression model in BigQuery ML.

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:14.870
This was not without
its challenges.

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:17.350
For example, we had to
either move our data sets

00:19:17.350 --> 00:19:19.030
into BigQuery, or
at least copy them

00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:21.880
as temporary tables in BigQuery
to build the final training

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:22.697
table.

00:19:22.697 --> 00:19:24.280
This means all the
feature engineering

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:27.062
has to be expressable as
SQL statements or UDFs.

00:19:27.062 --> 00:19:29.320
So for example, if you
use the cosine similarity

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:31.180
between vectors as
a feature, you'd

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:33.330
have to express that as a query.

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:37.300
Our approach was to build this
pipeline as a dependency graph.

00:19:37.300 --> 00:19:39.010
So what do I mean by that?

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:40.890
Basically, we've
created a data pipeline

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:44.040
entirely out of SQL queries
in BigQuery, organized it

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:45.450
into a graph.

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:47.160
So any extraction
transformations,

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.040
feature engineering,
et cetera, we

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:51.510
express as a directed graph
that creates temporary tables

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:52.487
at each step.

00:19:52.487 --> 00:19:54.820
This way, if anything were
to happen during the process,

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:56.790
we wouldn't have to run
the entire thing again.

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:58.620
It would just pick up
from the temporary tables

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:00.828
and then continue to run
the pipeline until we end up

00:20:00.828 --> 00:20:03.750
with the final
training data set.

00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:06.330
So I've already covered how
to train a logistic regression

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:09.505
model, so I'll just jump
to the final output.

00:20:09.505 --> 00:20:11.130
Basically, we end up
with a ranked list

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:13.410
of companies that have greater
likelihoods of becoming

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:14.898
a future customer.

00:20:14.898 --> 00:20:16.440
I do have to note
this screenshot was

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:17.970
lifted from our website.

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:20.577
This is not real data
being shown here.

00:20:20.577 --> 00:20:22.660
And again, because our
customers' entire marketing

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:25.030
and sales cycle could be
based around this list,

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:27.100
it's essential that
decisions made by the model

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:28.397
are explainable.

00:20:28.397 --> 00:20:30.230
The first thing a
customer will want to know

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:32.960
is, why did you
include this account?

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:35.330
Because BQ ML makes it easy
to extract feature weights,

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:37.490
for each model, we are,
in fact, able to make

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:39.740
explainable statements
like, well, this account

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:41.623
is similar to an
existing customer,

00:20:41.623 --> 00:20:43.790
or has a strong intent for
keywords in your industry

00:20:43.790 --> 00:20:47.030
or whatever.

00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:48.910
So for this use case,
we've been running

00:20:48.910 --> 00:20:51.060
the model in BQ ML
for months, and we've

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:53.920
had zero failures,
which was amazing.

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:55.910
Previous average times
of up to 40 minutes

00:20:55.910 --> 00:20:59.043
to run the job now take closer
to five minutes, so the time

00:20:59.043 --> 00:21:00.460
to create a new
list for customers

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:02.483
was reduced by a factor of 10.

00:21:02.483 --> 00:21:04.150
If you break down the
costs-- and again,

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:08.577
we've actually never had any
failures with BQ ML to date--

00:21:08.577 --> 00:21:10.410
the cost is now basically
only a few dollars

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:13.950
per job, which means a
cost reduction of over 10x.

00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:16.560
So overall, BigQuery ML has
been a huge game changer

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:18.630
for Demandbase,
not only improving

00:21:18.630 --> 00:21:21.060
the accuracy of our model
in the first use case,

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:23.610
but saving us a factor of
10x in both time and money

00:21:23.610 --> 00:21:25.212
for the second use case.

00:21:25.212 --> 00:21:27.170
So we absolutely plan on
continuing to leverage

00:21:27.170 --> 00:21:29.610
BQ ML for our next
generation of machine

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:31.890
learning models at Demandbase.

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:33.718
Thank you very much.

00:21:33.718 --> 00:21:35.760
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Thank
you, Eric, for your time.

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:40.170
These insights were
extremely useful.

00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:44.020
If you want to learn more,
we have a Coursera course

00:21:44.020 --> 00:21:48.840
that will teach you how to use
BigQuery ML with some code labs

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:50.640
using Qwiklabs.

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:52.290
They use cases that are shared--

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:56.590
we are building template
Jupyter notebooks,

00:21:56.590 --> 00:22:00.810
which we are hosting at
the link I'm sharing here.

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:04.920
And finally, if you
decide to use BigQuery ML

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:08.040
and have any questions
or feedback for our team,

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:10.450
please do write to us.

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:14.150
Thank you for your time, and
hope you have a great day.

